Explanation: Imports Python’s built-in heapq module. This module provides a binary min-heap implementation, which is essential for the greedy strategy. It allows us to always extract the two smallest elements efficiently in \(O(\log n)\) time
• class Node:: Defines a Blueprint for a tree node. Each node stores a char (character) and its freq (frequency).
• self.left and self.right: Pointers to connect this node to its left and right children in the binary tree.
• __lt__(self, other): Overloads the "less than" (<) operator. This tells the heapq module how to sort and compare two Node objects. It ensures that the min-heap arranges nodes solely based on their freq attribute.
self represents the specific instance of the class that you are currently creating or operating on. It is used to access the attributes (variables) and methods (functions) belonging to that specific object.
Think of a class as a blueprint and self as a pointer to the actual house built from that blueprint.
When you define a class, it acts as a generic template. You can use that template to create dozens of unique objects. Python needs a way to know which object's data you are trying to read or change. self clarifies that.
• heap = [...]: A list comprehension that iterates through your input dictionary (char_freqs) and wraps every key-value pair inside a new Node object.
• heapq.heapify(heap): Converts the list of nodes into a valid min-heap structure in-place (O(n) time). The node with the smallest frequency is now at index 0.
• while len(heap) > 1:: Runs continuously until only one single combined tree node remains in the heap.
• left_node = heapq.heappop(heap): Pops out (removes and returns) the node with the lowest frequency currently in the heap.
• right_node = heapq.heappop(heap): Pops out the next node, which has the second-lowest frequency.
• merged = Node(None, ...): Creates an internal parent node. It holds no character (None), but its frequency is the sum of the two popped children.
• merged.left = left_node / merged.right = right_node: Links the parent node to its two children, building the tree structure from the bottom up.
• heapq.heappush(heap, merged): Pushes this newly combined node back into the min-heap so it can be combined with other nodes in subsequent loops.
