Huffman Encoding is a popular algorithm used for lossless data compression. It reduces the size of data (like a text file) without losing a single bit of the original information.
It works by assigning variable-length binary codes to characters based on how frequently they appear:
• Frequent characters get shorter codes (fewer bits, e.g., e might become 0).
• Rare characters get longer codes (more bits, e.g., z might become 11010).
By using fewer bits for the characters we use most often, the total size of the file shrinks significantly.

The Key Feature: Prefix Codes

The Key Feature: Prefix Codes

Huffman codes are prefix codes. This means no code is a prefix (the start) of any other code. For example, if A is 0, no other character's code will start with 0. This makes the encoded binary stream unique and completely unambiguous to decode back into text without needing spaces between characters.

What is a Greedy Approach?

A Greedy Approach is an algorithmic strategy used to solve optimization problems by making the locally optimal choice at each step, with the hope that these local choices will lead to a globally optimal solution.
Think of it like a "short-sighted" strategy: you look only at the choices available right now, pick the best one immediately, and never reconsider or change your mind later.
• you just make the best immediate choice at every step.
Huffman Encoding relies completely on a greedy strategy to build its optimal tree structure. Here is how the greedy choice is made at every single step:

Step	What the Greedy Strategy Does
The Choice	It looks at all available characters/nodes and always picks the two with the absolute lowest frequencies.
The Action	It merges those two smallest nodes into a single parent node.
The Repeat	It places the new parent node back into the pile and repeats the exact same check until only one tree remains.

Phase / Operation	Time Complexity	Space Complexity
Heapify	\(\mathcal{O}(N)\)	\(\mathcal{O}(N)\)
Tree Building (Loop)	\(\mathcal{O}(N \log N)\)	\(\mathcal{O}(N)\)
Code Generation (DFS)	\(\mathcal{O}(N)\)	\(\mathcal{O}(H)\) (where H ≤ N)
Total Complexity	\(\mathcal{O}(N \log N)\)	\(\mathcal{O}(N)\)


